## Summary

Addresses the hang reported in #29594. The `google_web_search` tool waits for
its model request with no time limit. If the request stalls, or the server
keeps returning retryable errors, the agent stays on that one tool call for
minutes, or indefinitely, until the user cancels it.

Note: #29594 was closed by its reporter before a fix landed; the Nov 7 retest on
that thread confirmed that `WebSearchTool` on `main` still imposes no overall
deadline across retries.

This PR limits one web search to 60 seconds, retries included. At the limit the
tool returns a normal tool error, so the agent can carry on.

## Details

- `tools/web-search.ts`: a local `AbortController` + `setTimeout` forwards
  caller aborts and enforces a 60-second overall deadline (retries included).
  Using a local controller instead of `AbortSignal.any` keeps compatibility with
  Node.js `>=20.0.0`, avoids a type-assertion workaround, and lets `finally`
  clear the timer and caller listener without spuriously aborting the request
  signal on normal completion. Already-aborted caller signals return cancelled
  immediately. The call is awaited through a small new helper, `abortable()`, so
  the tool returns at the deadline even if a lower layer does not react to the
  signal.
  - Timeout: `WEB_SEARCH_FAILED` with
    `Web search for query "…" timed out after 60 seconds.`
  - Cancellation by the user is unchanged (`Web search was cancelled.`) and
    takes precedence over a timeout.
- `core/client.ts`: `GeminiClient.generateContent` now passes its abort signal
  to `retryWithBackoff`, as `BaseLlmClient` does since #30182. Without this, an
  aborted call keeps retrying in the background: with only the time limit in
  place, another request was sent 25 s after the tool had returned.
- `utils/abortable.ts`: the new helper, with tests.
- The limit is a file-local constant for now. Making it configurable can follow,
  possibly together with #30212. `web_fetch` waits in the same way and is not
  changed here.

## Related Issues

Related to #29594

## How to Validate

1. Unit tests:

   ```bash
   npm test -w @google/gemini-cli-core -- src/tools/web-search.test.ts \
     src/utils/abortable.test.ts src/core/client.test.ts
   ```

2. End to end, against a local fake API. It makes the model call
   `google_web_search`, then never answers the search (`MODE=stall`) or answers
   every attempt with a 503 (`MODE=503`). The script is at the end of this
   section.

   ```bash
   # Terminal 1
   MODE=stall node fake-gemini-api.mjs

   # Terminal 2: after `npm run build`, from an empty directory
   mkdir -p /tmp/repro-home/.gemini
   echo '{"security":{"auth":{"selectedType":"gemini-api-key"}}}' \
     > /tmp/repro-home/.gemini/settings.json
   cd "$(mktemp -d)"
   HOME=/tmp/repro-home GEMINI_API_KEY=dummy \
     GOOGLE_GEMINI_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8787 \
     GEMINI_API_HEADERS_TIMEOUT_MS=86400000 \
     node /path/to/gemini-cli/packages/cli --skip-trust --approval-mode=yolo \
     -o stream-json -p "search the web for anything"
   ```

   | Mode    | Before                              | After                                                                        |
   | ------- | ----------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
   | `stall` | The command never finishes          | A `tool_result` with "timed out after 60 seconds" at 60 s, then the run ends |
   | `503`   | The search runs for several minutes | The same error at 60 s; the fake API gets no more requests after that        |

   Why raise `GEMINI_API_HEADERS_TIMEOUT_MS` in the API-key repro? On the Code
   Assist / Google sign-in path (`gaxios`), the undici headers timeout does not
   apply at all; and even on the API-key path, `UND_ERR_HEADERS_TIMEOUT` is in
   `RETRYABLE_NETWORK_CODES` (`utils/retry.ts`), so without an overall tool
   deadline a stalling endpoint is still retried up to 10 times.

   <details>
   <summary>fake-gemini-api.mjs</summary>

   ```js
   // Fake Gemini API for reproducing the web search hang in Gemini CLI.
   //
   // Usage: MODE=stall|503|ok node fake-gemini-api.mjs   (PORT=8787 by default)
   //   stall  the search request is never answered
   //   503    every search attempt gets a 503 error
   //   ok     the search is answered at once
   //
   // The first chat turn asks the CLI to run `google_web_search`. Once the tool
   // result comes back, the next chat turn replies "done".
   import http from 'node:http';

   const MODE = process.env.MODE || 'stall';
   const PORT = Number(process.env.PORT || 8787);
   let searchRequests = 0;

   const answer = (part, extra = {}) => ({
     candidates: [
       { content: { role: 'model', parts: [part] }, finishReason: 'STOP', ...extra },
     ],
     usageMetadata: { promptTokenCount: 1, candidatesTokenCount: 1, totalTokenCount: 2 },
   });

   http
     .createServer((req, res) => {
       let body = '';
       req.on('data', (chunk) => (body += chunk));
       req.on('end', () => {
         const now = new Date().toISOString();
         let json = {};
         try {
           json = JSON.parse(body);
         } catch {
           // Not JSON: handled as a helper call below.
         }
         const send = (status, payload, stream = false) => {
           res.writeHead(status, {
             'content-type': stream ? 'text/event-stream' : 'application/json',
           });
           res.end(
             stream ? `data: ${JSON.stringify(payload)}\r\n\r\n` : JSON.stringify(payload),
           );
         };

         if (req.url.includes(':streamGenerateContent')) {
           // Main chat turn. Check the parsed request, not the raw text: the
           // system prompt itself contains the word "functionResponse".
           const parts = (json.contents ?? []).flatMap((c) => c.parts ?? []);
           const searched = parts.some((p) => p.functionResponse);
           console.log(now, 'chat turn ->', searched ? 'text' : 'google_web_search');
           const part = searched
             ? { text: 'done' }
             : { functionCall: { name: 'google_web_search', args: { query: 'repro' } } };
           send(200, answer(part), true);
         } else if ((json.tools ?? []).some((tool) => tool.googleSearch)) {
           // The web search request itself.
           console.log(now, `web-search request #${++searchRequests} (${MODE})`);
           if (MODE === '503') {
             send(503, { error: { code: 503, message: 'unavailable', status: 'UNAVAILABLE' } });
           } else if (MODE === 'ok') {
             send(
               200,
               answer(
                 { text: 'Example result text.' },
                 {
                   groundingMetadata: {
                     groundingChunks: [{ web: { uri: 'https://example.com', title: 'Example' } }],
                     groundingSupports: [
                       { segment: { startIndex: 0, endIndex: 7 }, groundingChunkIndices: [0] },
                     ],
                   },
                 },
               ),
             );
           }
           // MODE=stall: never answer.
         } else {
           // Other helper calls. The reply answers the model-routing question;
           // any other helper ignores it.
           console.log(now, 'other', req.method, req.url);
           const routing = { complexity_reasoning: 'fake API', complexity_score: 50 };
           send(200, answer({ text: JSON.stringify(routing) }));
         }
       });
     })
     .listen(PORT, '127.0.0.1', () =>
       console.log(`fake Gemini API on http://127.0.0.1:${PORT} (MODE=${MODE})`),
     );
   ```

   </details>

## Pre-Merge Checklist

- [x] Updated relevant documentation and README (if needed)
- [x] Added/updated tests (if needed)
- [x] Noted breaking changes (if any)
- [ ] Validated on required platforms/methods:
  - [ ] MacOS
    - [x] npm run
    - [ ] npx
    - [ ] Docker
    - [ ] Podman
    - [ ] Seatbelt
  - [ ] Windows
    - [ ] npm run
    - [ ] npx
    - [ ] Docker
  - [ ] Linux
    - [ ] npm run
    - [ ] npx
    - [ ] Docker

issues.body:
## Summary

Copilot CLI advertises MCP Tasks support during initialization, but it does not send task augmentation when invoking a tool declared with `taskSupport: "required"`. The server refuses the call and no task is started.

This reproduces with a generic MCP server. It does not depend on Atrium or any other project-specific implementation.

## Environment

- Copilot CLI: `1.0.115-2`
- Host: Windows
- MCP transport: stdio
- MCP SDK: `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk@1.30.0`
- MCP protocol: Tasks extension `io.modelcontextprotocol/tasks`

## Reproduction

1. Start a generic MCP stdio server that registers a task-based tool:

```ts
server.experimental.tasks.registerToolTask("delay_v2", {
  description: "Create a delayed task",
  execution: { taskSupport: "required" }
}, {
  createTask: async (args, extra) => {
    const task = await extra.taskStore.createTask({
      ttl: 300000,
      pollInterval: 1000
    });

    // Complete the task later and return a terminal result.
    return { task };
  },
  getTask: async (args, extra) => {
    return extra.taskStore.getTask(extra.taskId);
  },
  getTaskResult: async (args, extra) => {
    return extra.taskStore.getTaskResult(extra.taskId);
  }
});
```

2. Configure this server as an additional MCP server.
3. Ask Copilot CLI to call `delay_v2` once.

## Observed behavior

Copilot CLI initializes the MCP connection and reports a client capability containing:

```json
{
  "extensions": {
    "io.modelcontextprotocol/tasks": {}
  }
}
```

It does not send an augmented `tools/call` request. Instead, the server receives a normal `tools/call` request without the task augmentation. A server implementing `taskSupport: "required"` rejects it with an MCP error:

```text
MCP error -32601: delay_v2 requires task augmentation (taskSupport: 'required')
```

No task ID is created, no task status is polled or notified, and no result is returned.

## Expected behavior

For a task-required tool, Copilot CLI should send the supported MCP Tasks request shape, including task augmentation, and handle the returned task lifecycle.

After receiving a task handle, Copilot CLI should support one of:

- polling `tasks/get` until a terminal status is reached, or
- subscribing to `notifications/tasks/status` and processing the terminal task result.

At minimum, Copilot CLI should not advertise `io.modelcontextprotocol/tasks` support unless it can correctly initiate a task-augmented tool call.

## Control result

An SDK 1.30.0 MCP client and the same generic server successfully completed a 30-second task using the Tasks protocol: task creation, working status polling, a terminal task-status notification, and terminal result retrieval all worked.

This isolates the failure to the Copilot CLI host’s task augmentation behavior rather than the task server implementation.

## References

- MCP Tasks extension: https://tasks.extensions.modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/tasks
- MCP 2026-07-28 Streamable HTTP: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/draft/basic/transports/streamable-http
- MCP 2026-07-28 specification: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28

### What happened?

`consumeSseStream()` in `packages/mcp/src/transports/streamable-http.ts` keeps the pending event in one string:

```ts
buffered += text;
...
buffered = buffered.slice(start);
```

When a large event arrives as many chunks without a line boundary, every chunk appends to that string and `consumeLines()` rescans the whole accumulated prefix. This is the same quadratic pattern that #11761 fixed for the stdio line reader.

Measured on `a9878e1` (1.5.2), a single `data:` line delivered in 64 KiB chunks:

| event size | time |
| --- | --- |
| 1 MiB | 24.4 ms |
| 4 MiB | 288.6 ms |
| 8 MiB | 1107 ms |
| 16 MiB | 4507 ms |

`JsonRpcLineReader` handles a 16 MiB incomplete message in 4.9 ms.

`DEFAULT_MAX_MESSAGE_BYTES` is 16 MiB, so a large MCP tool result can block the event loop for several seconds. 4 MiB already takes ~290 ms.

### Steps to reproduce

Node 24, repo root, `--experimental-strip-types`. Feed `consumeSseStream` a stream that writes `data: `, then N bytes in 64 KiB chunks, then `\n\n`:

```ts
import { consumeSseStream } from "./packages/mcp/src/transports/streamable-http.ts";

const total = 16 * 1024 * 1024;
const chunk = new Uint8Array(64 * 1024).fill(0x61);
let sent = 0;
const stream = new ReadableStream({
	pull(controller) {
		if (sent === 0) { controller.enqueue(new TextEncoder().encode("data: ")); sent = 6; return; }
		if (sent < total) { controller.enqueue(chunk); sent += chunk.length; return; }
		controller.enqueue(new TextEncoder().encode("\n\n"));
		controller.close();
	},
});

const t0 = performance.now();
await consumeSseStream(stream, { maxEventBytes: total + 1024, onEvent: () => {} });
console.log(performance.now() - t0);
```

Vary `total` over 1/4/8/16 MiB; the time grows quadratically.

### Expected behavior

Parsing should be linear in event size. Suggested fix: keep the incomplete event as a chunk queue and scan only new bytes, joining once at the line boundary — the approach `JsonRpcLineReader` already uses. `readBodyText()` (`text += decoder.decode(...)`) has the same pattern for large JSON responses.

I can submit a fix if this is welcome.

Version: 1.5.2 (`a9878e1`)

# [Bug] Inter-session transport: empty envelope events + completion echo into source session

## Context
- Version: 2026.11.6 (dist bundle)
- Symptoms observed in production over multiple weeks (instances on Sep 22 and Oct 1, 2026; re-confirmed Nov 2026):

### Symptom 1 - empty envelopes as separate events
When an inter-session message is announced (sessions_send / subagent_announce flow), the target/requester session sometimes receives SEPARATE events containing only routing metadata - `[Inter-session message] sourceSession=... sourceChannel=... sourceTool=... isUser=false` plus the runtime explanation - with **no payload content attached** (5+ occurrences observed, with a GO-vs-ping ordering race between envelope and payload).

Relevant code (dist 2026.11.6):
- `dist/input-provenance-*.mjs` -> `buildInterSessionPromptContext()` / `annotateInterSessionPromptText()`: the envelope header is built from provenance alone; an empty/whitespace payload passes the `!e.trim()` guard and the callers still enqueue the (now empty) annotated text as a system event.
- `dist/sessions-spawn-tool-*.mjs` -> `notifySessionsSendSession()`: `enqueueSystemEventEntry(annotateInterSessionPromptText(e.message, e.inputProvenance), ...)` enqueues without checking whether the annotated payload is empty -> header-only / empty events.
- `dist/subagent-announce-*.mjs` -> `buildDescendantWakeMessage()`: joins `..., "", e.findings`; when `findings` is empty/blank the wake event is envelope-only ("descendant settled" metadata, no content).

### Symptom 2 - completion echo into the source session
The final reply of a run started by an inter-session send gets re-enqueued in the source (requester) session - including duplicate announces of the same completion (ordering race), producing a bidirectional echo: the source session wakes, processes the echoed reply, and may answer again, which triggers another announce. Current user-space workaround: treat empty envelopes as no-ops, check `sessions_history` before re-sending, use `mode:"followup"` for acks.

Root cause (delivery layer):
- `dist/subagent-announce-delivery-*.mjs` -> `deliverSubagentAnnouncementBound()`: completion announce delivery is not idempotent at the (requester session, idempotency key) level - a retried/racing announce for the same key is enqueued again into the requester session.

## Proposed minimal fix
Three localized guards (applied and running locally; no behavior change for well-formed deliveries):

1. `notifySessionsSendSession()` (sessions-send delivery): compute the annotated text first; if blank, do not enqueue - log `dropped empty inter-session notify envelope` and return `status:"skipped", reason:"empty_payload_envelope_suppressed"`.
2. `buildDescendantWakeMessage()` (subagent announce): if `findings` is blank, merge an explicit marker line into the wake text instead of emitting an envelope-only event.
3. `deliverSubagentAnnouncementBound()` (announce delivery): module-level Set keyed by `targetRequesterSessionKey|directIdempotencyKey`; a duplicate announce returns `{delivered:false, path:"none", reason:"duplicate_announce_echo_suppressed", terminal:true, disposition:"intentional_non_delivery"}` + subsystem log warn.

```diff
--- a/dist/sessions-spawn-tool-*.mjs (notifySessionsSendSession)
 async function notifySessionsSendSession(e) {
+  const __annotated = annotateInterSessionPromptText(e.message, e.inputProvenance);
+  if (!__annotated || !__annotated.trim()) {
+    console.warn("[fix] dropped empty inter-session notify envelope");
+    return jsonResult({ runId: e.runId, status: "skipped", sessionKey: e.displayKey, reason: "empty_payload_envelope_suppressed" });
+  }
   const t = resolveGatewayToolOperatorSelection();
   ...

--- a/dist/subagent-announce-*.mjs (buildDescendantWakeMessage)
   Task: ${e.taskLabel},
   "",
-  e.findings
+  e.findings && String(e.findings).trim() ? e.findings : "(no findings payload returned - envelope-only event suppressed)"
 ].join("\n");

--- a/dist/subagent-announce-delivery-*.mjs (deliverSubagentAnnouncementBound)
+const __echoGuardSeen = new Set();
 async function deliverSubagentAnnouncementBound(e) {
   const sourceOwnerChanged = () => ...;
   if (sourceOwnerChanged()) return sourceOwnerChangedResult();
+  const __key = `${e.targetRequesterSessionKey ?? ""}|${e.directIdempotencyKey ?? ""}`;
+  if (e.targetRequesterSessionKey && e.directIdempotencyKey) {
+    if (__echoGuardSeen.has(__key)) {
+      log.warn("[fix] suppressed duplicate inter-session completion announce (echo guard)");
+      return { delivered: false, path: "none", reason: "duplicate_announce_echo_suppressed", terminal: true, disposition: "intentional_non_delivery" };
+    }
+    __echoGuardSeen.add(__key);
+  }
```

## Test that reproduces
1. Unit: call `annotateInterSessionPromptText("", {kind:"inter_session", sourceSessionKey:"agent:a:main", sourceTool:"sessions_send"})` -> ""; then assert `notifySessionsSendSession` with that message/inputProvenance enqueues nothing and returns `status:"skipped"` (pre-patch: an empty system event entry is created).
2. Unit: `buildDescendantWakeMessage({taskLabel:"T", findings:""})` -> output contains the explicit no-findings marker, not a trailing empty section (pre-patch: envelope-only text).
3. Repro/integration: session A `sessions_send` -> session B; force the completion announce delivery twice with the same `directIdempotencyKey` (e.g. retry the announce worker after partial failure). Pre-patch: requester session accumulates 2+ announce events (echo/duplicates); post-patch: second delivery is suppressed with `duplicate_announce_echo_suppressed` and logged.

## Notes / limitations
- The echo guard is in-memory (module-level Set): it deduplicates within a gateway process lifetime; cross-restart dedup would require persisting the idempotency keys (we intentionally kept the patch minimal).
- Fix validated locally on 2026.11.6 dist with `node --check` on all three files; smoke inter-session check planned after gateway restart.

*Reported by a self-hosted operator running a multi-agent fleet (dozens of inter-session sends/day). Happy to provide more traces or a PR.*

### Bug type

Behavior bug (incorrect output/state without crash)

### Beta release blocker

No

### Summary

For a user without the admin scope, the New session place picker shows "No folders match your search." when they type the path of an existing folder in a different Unicode spelling than the one `fs.realpath` returns. On macOS many accented folder names are stored decomposed (NFD) while typed text is precomposed (NFC), and non-admin directory requests are resolved with `fs.realpath` before they are listed, so the picker receives the on-disk spelling and compares it byte for byte with what was typed.

Admins are not affected. Their requests are listed from the typed path as it was sent, so both sides carry the same spelling.

The empty result comes from an intentional branch, not from a missing check. Typed loads keep the requested spelling on purpose: the comment at `ui/src/pages/new-session/place-browser-state.ts:155-156` states that a typed path the Gateway canonicalized to something else intentionally shows "No matching folders" instead of children. That branch and the directory comparison behind it came in with #139417; the typed-versus-navigation handling was reworked in #165446. It was written for symlinked paths under write scope. A Unicode respelling by `realpath` looks the same to the picker.

This is the single issue obviyus asked for when closing #191694 and #191720: "Please open one issue (or one PR at the shared owner) that covers the whole pattern". Those two PRs are superseded; see Additional information. Search-box matching is tracked in #189517 and is not part of this issue.

### Steps to reproduce

I do not have a macOS machine. I have not driven the real UI and I have not observed `fs.realpath` respelling a path on macOS. What follows was executed on `origin/main` at `0174cf4d0083`, from the repo root with `node --import ./scripts/tsx.mjs`, against the shipped functions.

**1. The Gateway echoes the requested spelling.** For a client with the admin scope, `fs.listDir` lists the typed path as it was sent (`src/gateway/server-methods/fs.ts:90-93`). For everyone else it first calls `resolveWorkspacePathContainment`, which runs `fs.realpath` on the request (`fs.ts:94-107`, `src/gateway/server-methods/workspace-path-containment.ts:15`), and lists that result.

Both paths go through `listHostDirectories`, which is lexical. Called with either spelling, it returns that same spelling:

```
listing.path echoes the requested NFC spelling: true
listing.path echoes the requested NFD spelling: true
```

<details>
<summary>Script for step 1</summary>

```ts
import { mkdir, mkdtemp, rm } from "node:fs/promises";
import os from "node:os";
import path from "node:path";
import { listHostDirectories } from "./src/infra/host-directory-listing.ts";

const word = "R\u00e9sum\u00e9s";
const nfc = word.normalize("NFC");
const nfd = word.normalize("NFD");
if (nfc === nfd) throw new Error("expected two distinct spellings");

const base = await mkdtemp(path.join(os.tmpdir(), "nfd-listing-"));
try {
  const result: Record<string, boolean> = {};
  for (const [label, spelling] of [["NFC", nfc], ["NFD", nfd]] as const) {
    const dir = path.join(base, spelling);
    await mkdir(path.join(dir, "Projects"), { recursive: true });
    const listing = await listHostDirectories(dir);
    result[label] = listing.path === path.resolve(dir) && listing.path.includes(spelling);
  }
  console.log("listing.path echoes the requested NFC spelling:", result.NFC);
  console.log("listing.path echoes the requested NFD spelling:", result.NFD);
} finally {
  await rm(base, { recursive: true, force: true });
}
```

</details>

So the two sides of the picker can only diverge on the non-admin path, when `realpath` answers with a different spelling.

**2. Given the two spellings, the picker comparison fails.** `listing` below models what the non-admin path returns for that folder when `realpath` answers with the decomposed spelling.

```ts
// Run from the repo root:  node --import ./scripts/tsx.mjs repro-place-picker.mts
import { isKnownWorkspacePath, sameAbsolutePath } from "./ui/src/pages/new-session/path.ts";
import { resolvePlaceBrowserView } from "./ui/src/pages/new-session/place-browser-view.ts";

// One folder name, two Unicode spellings.
const word = "R\u00e9sum\u00e9s";
const nfc = `/Users/me/Documents/${word.normalize("NFC")}`; // precomposed: typed or pasted
const nfd = `/Users/me/Documents/${word.normalize("NFD")}`; // decomposed: spelling on disk on macOS
// Guard against comparing two different words by accident.
if (nfc === nfd || nfc.normalize("NFC") !== nfd.normalize("NFC")) {
  throw new Error("probe is invalid: nfc and nfd are not canonically equivalent");
}

const listing = {
  path: nfd,
  entries: [
    { name: "Projects", path: `${nfd}/Projects`, kind: "directory" },
    { name: "Notes", path: `${nfd}/Notes`, kind: "directory" },
  ],
};
const view = (draft: string) => {
  const { entries, empty } = resolvePlaceBrowserView({ listing, draft, loading: false });
  return `[${entries.map((entry) => entry.name).join(", ")}] empty=${empty}`;
};

console.log(`nfc.length=${nfc.length} nfd.length=${nfd.length}`);
console.log("sameAbsolutePath(nfc, nfd)                    =", sameAbsolutePath(nfc, nfd));
console.log("sameAbsolutePath(nfd, nfd)      (control)     =", sameAbsolutePath(nfd, nfd));
console.log("isKnownWorkspacePath([nfc], nfd/proj)         =", isKnownWorkspacePath([nfc], `${nfd}/proj`));
console.log("isKnownWorkspacePath([nfc], nfc/proj) (ctrl)  =", isKnownWorkspacePath([nfc], `${nfc}/proj`));
console.log("view(nfd + '/')                 (control)     =", view(`${nfd}/`));
console.log("view(nfc + '/')                               =", view(`${nfc}/`));
console.log("view(nfc + '/Pro')                            =", view(`${nfc}/Pro`));
console.log("view(nfd + '/projects')        (control)      =", view(`${nfd}/projects`));
```

In the real UI this corresponds to a non-admin pasting or retyping the path of an accented folder instead of clicking into it. That step is derived from the code and was not executed.

### Expected behavior

Both spellings name the same directory, so the picker lists that directory's entries.

### Actual behavior

Output of the script in step 2. The first line shows the two spellings are not byte-identical. The four lines marked `(control)` or `(ctrl)` compare a spelling with itself and are all right. The other four compare the two spellings of the same folder and are all wrong.

```
nfc.length=27 nfd.length=29
sameAbsolutePath(nfc, nfd)                    = false
sameAbsolutePath(nfd, nfd)      (control)     = true
isKnownWorkspacePath([nfc], nfd/proj)         = false
isKnownWorkspacePath([nfc], nfc/proj) (ctrl)  = true
view(nfd + '/')                 (control)     = [Projects, Notes] empty=none
view(nfc + '/')                               = [] empty=no-matches
view(nfc + '/Pro')                            = [] empty=no-matches
view(nfd + '/projects')        (control)      = [Projects] empty=none
```

`empty === "no-matches"` is rendered as `newSession.browserNoMatches`, "No folders match your search." (`ui/src/pages/new-session/place-browser.tsx:148-156`).

Cause: `comparableAbsolutePath` in `ui/src/pages/new-session/path.ts:17-40` handles separators, `.`/`..` and Windows drive case, but never looks at the Unicode form. It backs `sameAbsolutePath` (four call sites: `place-browser-state.ts:72-73` and `place-browser-view.ts:60-67`) and `isKnownWorkspacePath`.

### OpenClaw version

2026.11.7 (`origin/main` at `0174cf4d0083`)

### Operating system

Verified at function level on Windows 11. The user-facing symptom depends on `fs.realpath` returning a different spelling than the one typed, which is a macOS condition.

### Install method

Source checkout

### Model

N/A (Control UI only)

### Provider / routing chain

N/A

### Screenshots, recordings, and evidence

Function output above. #187988 (merged) made the neighbouring entry-name filter canonical and added decomposed-name cases to `ui/src/pages/new-session/place-browser-view.test.ts`. Those cases exercise entry names only. None of them types a directory path in the other spelling, which is why this gap was not caught.

### Impact and severity

Affected: users without the admin scope on macOS, whose folder path contains a character with different composed and decomposed spellings, where `fs.realpath` returns a different spelling than the one they typed, and who type or paste that path into the New session place picker instead of clicking into the folder. NOT_ENOUGH_INFO on how common that is in practice: I have not observed it on macOS.

Severity: low. The suggestion list is empty for a folder that has subfolders. This is a client-side check only ("the Gateway remains the realpath authority", per the comment on `isKnownWorkspacePath`), so the Gateway does not allow or deny any path wrongly.

Frequency: deterministic in the executed functions (4 of 4 cross-spelling cases wrong, 4 of 4 same-spelling controls right).

Consequence: the folder's subfolders cannot be browsed from the picker. The typed path itself still works, because "Use this folder" stays enabled for an absolute path (`ui/src/pages/new-session/place-browser.tsx:213`). Clicking into the folder also works.

### Additional information

**Other users of the same comparison (read, not reproduced end to end).**

- `ui/src/pages/new-session/draft-place-browser.ts:374-379` consults `isKnownWorkspacePath` only for non-admins, to drop recent folders that fall outside the known workspaces. A recent folder could be hidden the same way. I did not exercise that through the real list.
- `ui/src/pages/skills/library-dialog-content.tsx:254-256` decides whether a new file already exists with `file.path === path`. Typing the name in the other spelling makes `exists` false, so instead of showing `skillLibrary.fileExists` the dialog appends a second draft entry with empty content. On macOS both entries name the same file. I did not verify what the save then writes.

**Why this is an issue and not a PR.** Two decisions are involved.

Folding paths to one Unicode form is not a pure fix. On Linux a composed and a decomposed name can be two different directories, and the backend keeps them distinct on purpose: `src/agents/agent-scope.test.ts` has a Linux-only case, "keeps distinct Unicode workspace directories under their own agents", that creates both and expects them to resolve to different agents.

The empty result also sits in that intentional branch (#139417, reworked in #165446), and I did not want to change its behaviour without your call.

Options I can see for the two UI sites:

1. Fold unconditionally. Right on macOS. Wrong on Linux, where the place picker would list the other directory's entries and the skill dialog would report a false `fileExists`.
2. Fold only when the browser runs on macOS (`process.platform === "darwin"`). Leaves Linux alone, but the browser's platform need not match the platform of the host being browsed, and it misses normalization-insensitive volumes elsewhere.
3. Ask the filesystem whether two spellings resolve to the same entry, as the backend already does for missing-path aliases in `src/state/openclaw-agent-db.paths.ts` (`probePathSuffixAliasesSync`).

If one of these directions works for you, I am happy to implement both sites in a single PR.

**Status of the superseded PRs.**

- #191720 (workspace rail browser search): the search moved into `ui/src/pages/chat/components/chat-session-workspace-catalog.ts` and has been normalized on both sides since #187036. Nothing is left to fix.
- #191694 (file preview search): the filter in `ui/src/components/file-preview-modal.tsx:68-73` is still un-normalized, but its only production caller passes `layout="document"` without a query, so that branch is not reachable today.

**Not verified, for completeness.** From reading the code, several backend containment checks appear to use the lexical `isPathInside` on strings that were not resolved through the filesystem together, which could produce the same mismatch. I did not reproduce any of those and am not claiming they are bugs.

**Related, not duplicates.** #189517 covers search-box text matching (Greek sigma) and already has a linked PR. #193585 covers the config-driven directory query.

**Revision note.** An earlier version of this issue did not limit the symptom to sessions without the admin scope and said the folder could not be picked by typing its path. Both were wrong and are corrected above.

