### Screenshots, recordings, and evidence

<img width="1383" height="892" alt="Знімок екрана 2026-11-23 232812" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/1b17fc2b-5abd-4ab3-965e-8108bfcafcd7" />

### Impact and severity

- **Affected users/systems/channels:** Observed on Windows 10.0.26300 (64-bit) with WSL 2 and the packaged Companion (`OpenClawFoundation.OpenClaw_rfcbke2p71se2`). The Windows PowerShell execution policy is set to AllSigned via Group Policy ("Allow only signed scripts"). The affected path is **Install a local Gateway (WSL)**. No messaging channels are involved: setup fails before the Gateway is installed.
- **Severity:** Blocks workflow. The local WSL Gateway cannot be set up: setup fails at step `wsl-create` and rolls back. No data risk observed: rollback ran `wsl --terminate` / `--unregister` only for `OpenClawGateway`, and the pre-existing distros (`Ubuntu`, `docker-desktop`) were not touched.
- **Frequency:** Always on this configuration. This is an execution-policy rejection of an unsigned script (`cleanup.ps1 ... is not digitally signed`, `UnauthorizedAccess`), not an intermittent error.
- **Consequence:** Failed onboarding. Each attempt:
  1. downloads and installs Ubuntu 24.04 (`wsl --install ... --web-download`, ~60 s);
  2. runs `powershell.exe ... -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File cleanup.ps1 -Register`, which exits with code 1 after ~10 s;
  3. fails the step after ~70 s, then unregisters the new distro and deletes the empty `wsl\` directory.

  No local Gateway remains after rollback.

### Additional information

**Precondition:** OpenClaw Companion installed as a packaged app (MSIX `OpenClawFoundation.OpenClaw`, version `<version>` from `(Get-AppxPackage OpenClawFoundation.OpenClaw).Version`). WSL 2 with support for `wsl --install --name ... --location ...`.

**1. Enforce "Allow only signed scripts" for Windows PowerShell 5.1**

Companion runs `%SystemRoot%\System32\WindowsPowerShell\v1.0\powershell.exe`, so the *Windows PowerShell* policy applies (not the PowerShell 7 / "PowerShell Core" template).

Option A – Local Group Policy Editor (Pro / Enterprise / Education):
1. Press Win+R and run `gpedit.msc` as an administrator.
2. Go to **Computer Configuration → Administrative Templates → Windows Components → Windows PowerShell**.
3. Open **Turn on Script Execution**, select **Enabled**, set **Execution Policy** to **Allow only signed scripts**, click **OK**.
4. In an elevated terminal run `gpupdate /force`.

Option B – equivalent registry policy (any edition, including Home), elevated terminal:
```
reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\PowerShell" /v EnableScripts /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f
reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\PowerShell" /v ExecutionPolicy /t REG_SZ /d AllSigned /f
```

**2. Verify** in a new PowerShell window:
```powershell
Get-ExecutionPolicy -List   # MachinePolicy = AllSigned
```
Optional check without Companion (`-ExecutionPolicy Bypass` cannot override the policy):
```powershell
Set-Content "$env:TEMP\probe.ps1" 'Write-Output ok'
powershell.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "$env:TEMP\probe.ps1"
$LASTEXITCODE   # 1 – "...probe.ps1 is not digitally signed. You cannot run this script on the current system."
```

**3. Trigger the bug**
1. Launch OpenClaw Companion and open setup (first-run wizard or the **Gateway Setup** shortcut).
2. Choose **Install a local Gateway (WSL)** and start setup.
3. Step **Create WSL instance** (`wsl-create`): `wsl.exe --install --distribution Ubuntu-24.04 --name OpenClawGateway ... --no-launch --web-download` succeeds (exit 0). Then `powershell.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File %USERPROFILE%\.openclaw-companion\cleanup\<PackageFamilyName>\cleanup.ps1 ... -Register -CreatedDistro ...` exits with code 1. The step fails with `InvalidOperationException: Automatic Gateway cleanup could not be registered. ... cleanup.ps1 is not digitally signed`, and setup rolls back (`wsl --unregister OpenClawGateway`).

**Revert the test policy**
- Option A: set **Turn on Script Execution** back to **Not Configured**, then `gpupdate /force`.
- Option B:
```
reg delete "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\PowerShell" /v EnableScripts /f
reg delete "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\PowerShell" /v ExecutionPolicy /f
```

prs.body:
## What Problem This Solves

Slack system events can arrive without a known conversation type when metadata lookup fails. Treating an ambiguous conversation as a regular channel can apply channel permissions to a group DM.

## User Impact

User impact: reaction, pin, membership, edit/delete, and interaction events are rejected when their conversation type cannot be resolved. Installations missing conversation-read scopes may see these events dropped; grant the manifest’s `channels:read`, `groups:read`, and `mpim:read` scopes to restore metadata lookup.

## Why This Change Was Made

Require a conversation type from event metadata, cached metadata, or `conversations.info` before authorizing non-direct-message system events. Keep direct-message authorization and known-channel behavior intact.

## Evidence

At `a15b610030cc57c91005c446e652b2da7bed83e9`, the new regression case rejects an unresolved C-prefixed conversation with `ambiguous-channel-type`; it failed against the previous implementation. Known channel and group-DM authorization controls pass alongside event-handler and ingress coverage.

- `pnpm test extensions/slack/src/monitor/auth.test.ts extensions/slack/src/monitor/events extensions/slack/src/monitor/events.enterprise.test.ts extensions/slack/src/monitor/ingress.test.ts extensions/slack/src/monitor/ingress.interaction-drain.test.ts extensions/slack/src/monitor/provider.interop.test.ts`: 14 files, 236 tests passed.
- `pnpm tsgo:extensions` and `pnpm tsgo:extensions:test`: passed.
- Focused oxlint with `--openclaw-focused-config`, formatting checks on all four changed files, and `git diff --check`: passed.

Evidence uses local production authorization and event adapters with synthetic Slack fixtures. A live Slack workspace was not exercised.

### Shipped Gateway HTTP proof

The actual `pnpm openclaw gateway run` CLI was started on an isolated loopback port with a fresh temporary state directory, the real Slack plugin and Bolt HTTP receiver, and the supported `SLACK_API_URL` pointed at a local Slack Web API fixture. Signed Slack HTTP callbacks crossed the real receiver, durable ingress, authorization and routing code. A read-only observer recorded the actual product-owned system-event queue; it never injected or replaced authorization, handlers, routing, or enqueue operations.

Command: `node .artifacts/slack-conversation-type-proof/gateway-http.mjs` (the driver starts `pnpm openclaw gateway run --port <isolated-port> --verbose`). Result at `a15b610030cc57c91005c446e652b2da7bed83e9`:

| Signed HTTP callback | HTTP acknowledgment | Actual system-event queue delta | Authorization result |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| cold unresolved C-prefixed conversation | 200 | 0 | ambiguous-channel-type |
| cold unresolved G-prefixed conversation | 200 | 0 | ambiguous-channel-type |
| known channel outsider retains open policy | 200 | 1 | allowed |
| known C-prefixed MPIM outsider | 200 | 0 | sender-not-allowlisted |
| known C-prefixed MPIM owner | 200 | 1 | allowed |
| direct-message configured owner | 200 | 1 | allowed |

All six scenarios passed. The denied cases added zero events; the three controls each added one real system event to their routed session. The owner allowlist and open-channel policy remained intact. The Gateway shut down cleanly after SIGTERM and the local API fixture closed. The HTTP 200s alone are acknowledgments; the queue deltas and rejection logs establish the product result.

