Related: #167280. Follow-up to #173580.

## What Problem This Solves

A Codex follow-up can spend its entire startup budget waiting for a retired stdio process that another session still uses. If the target thread's release stays unconfirmed, startup cleanup can then close a healthy replacement client shared by other sessions.

## User Impact

The handoff now reports a timeout after the existing ten-second thread-unload confirmation budget (distinct from the five-second unsubscribe-request budget). A failure before a thread write leaves the replacement client available for a later attempt. The timeout tells the user to retry the message shortly or continue in a new chat if the conversation still cannot resume. Resuming on another writer still requires proof that the old process exited or the exact thread unloaded.

## Why This Change Was Made

The process-exit wait already respected caller cancellation. This change uses the existing timeout helper and pre-write cancellation callback at that same wait. A shorter whole-startup deadline still controls startup cleanup. The change adds no config, transport, or new source of ownership state. Native Codex acknowledges unsubscribe separately from shutdown and emits `thread/closed` after teardown completes. [Native teardown at 0.164.0](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/rust-v0.164.0/codex-rs/app-server/src/request_processors/thread_lifecycle.rs#L559-L623)

## Evidence

### CI fixture repair (`3d134196a0583`)

The conflict-repaired head failed `checks-node-changed-extensions-bundle-3` in `thread-lifecycle.native-config.test.ts`: its policy-change fixture expected already-admitted work to be revoked. Main's admission change in #202513 snapshots operator policy, including through the public plugin test host. The fixture now supplies the restricted policy at admission and retains both rejection assertions: a visible model-policy error and no native thread/turn dispatch. Missing-issuer coverage remains. Production code and the ten-second handoff/seven-second closure regression are unchanged.

Focused static lint, pinned formatter/pre-commit, and diff checks passed. A local baseline test attempt could not reach tests because the shared dependency installation lacks the TypeScript `./fs` export. No passing local runtime or typecheck claim for this repair; fresh fork CI is required. Prior-head CI failed in this one test shard; all other non-aggregate jobs passed or were intentionally skipped.

### Shutdown budget follow-up (`071f3c870422e`)

The retired-owner handoff now shares the existing ten-second thread-unload confirmation constant with configuration adoption. This avoids rejecting legitimate slow shutdowns while keeping bounded failure and healthy-client preservation. Unsubscribe RPCs retain their five-second limit; shorter whole-startup deadlines still take precedence.

A new production-startup-boundary regression delivers `thread/closed` after seven simulated seconds while a peer keeps the old process alive. It fails with `timed_out` on `c3ebf5981fc4` and passes with this follow-up, with no resume before closure. Startup and adoption suites: **29 tests passed**, 34.52 seconds wrapper wall time (30.75 seconds Vitest). Native seven-second teardown was not independently reproduced; this delayed-closure proof uses the existing synthetic native transport. Earlier native traces below remain evidence for their explicitly named older heads, not the updated ten-second timing. Current-head CI timing is pending.

The added test protects successful slow shutdown, which the never-closes timeout controls do not cover. The separate test-line allocation grants exactly 21 net lines for this regression.

Focused lint, extension production typecheck, formatting, and diff checks passed. Local ClawSweeper reviewed `c3ebf5981fc4..071f3c870422e` and found no actionable correctness or security defects.

### Earlier-head evidence

**Recorded native stdio trace on `87ddb73527d5` (macOS arm64, Codex 0.164.0).** The managed launch used `thread_unload_delay_secs=0`. In the existing [native test fixture](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/87ddb73527d5a7d900053e88bfdab9eebbea76d1/extensions/codex/src/app-server/thread-lifecycle.handoff.native.test.ts#L163), two app-server processes ran on stdio with disposable state and a loopback model. The following is terminal output from the deliberately unconfirmed sibling handoff. The old process answered for the same loaded thread *after* rejection. The same replacement client issued zero resume requests during the wait and then completed a fresh turn on its already-resumed conversation.

```text
HANDOFF {"reason":"timed_out","message":"The previous Codex thread did not confirm shutdown before the handoff timed out. Try sending your message again in a few seconds. If the conversation still cannot resume, start a new chat.","elapsedMs":5001,"successorResumes":0}
OLD_OWNER {"method":"thread/read","sameThread":true,"status":"idle"}
REPLACEMENT {"method":"turn/start","terminalStatus":"completed","providerRequests":4}
 Test Files  2 passed (2)
      Tests  23 passed (23)
```

The startup regression separately exercises caller cleanup and checks that the healthy client's input stays open. To repeat the native trace, apply the patch below to the test fixture at `87ddb73527d5` (the current head instead uses ten seconds). The provider count includes earlier fixture turns.

